    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING',
      content:
          '**Psychiatric Stability Assessment:**\n- The psychiatric clearance for rehabilitation means the patient is no longer in the acute phase of suicidal crisis — it does NOT mean she is psychiatrically stable or at low risk\n- **Current risk level: Moderate-High.** Risk factors include: recent high-lethality attempt, prior attempt, active passive suicidal ideation, borderline personality traits, recent relationship loss, chronic depression, self-injury history, ambivalence about survival, and new catastrophic disability\n- **Protective factors:** Called 911 herself (ambivalence about death, help-seeking behavior), mother involved, young age (greater neuroplasticity for adjustment), some prior therapy engagement, medications initiated\n\n**Suicide Risk Screening Tools:**\n- **Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS):** Gold standard for structured risk assessment. Should be administered at admission and repeated at regular intervals (weekly minimum). Distinguishes passive ideation, active ideation without plan, active ideation with plan, and behavior.\n- **PHQ-9 Item 9:** Screens for suicidal ideation but is not sufficient alone. Her score of 18 with positive item 9 warrants comprehensive assessment.\n- **Beck Hopelessness Scale:** Hopelessness is the strongest predictor of future suicide attempts — her score of 14 (moderate) is concerning and should be tracked over time.\n- **SAFE-T (Suicide Assessment Five-Step Triage):** Structured framework for ongoing risk assessment.\n\n**Medication Safety:**\n- **Fluoxetine:** Appropriate choice — evidence base for depression AND for reducing impulsive aggression in borderline personality. Black box warning for increased suicidality in young adults (<25) — monitor closely during first 4-8 weeks. She is 22, within the warning range.\n- **Quetiapine:** Sedation benefit for sleep, mood stabilization benefit for BPD traits, low lethality in overdose compared to other options. Appropriate choice.\n- **Gabapentin:** Low overdose lethality, useful for neuropathic pain. Appropriate.\n- **Acetaminophen:** Given her prior overdose with acetaminophen, this medication must be strictly controlled — dispensed by nursing per dose, no bottles at bedside, pharmacy-flagged for quantity limits.\n- **Medications to AVOID:** Tricyclic antidepressants (lethal in overdose), large quantities of any medication accessible to patient, medications requiring sharp objects for administration (injectable formulations when possible alternatives exist).',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ Diagnostic question',
      content:
          '**Q: She denies suicidal ideation on the C-SSRS but her therapist reports she seems "disconnected" and "going through the motions." Is she safe?**\n\n**A:** Denial of suicidal ideation on a structured screening tool does NOT guarantee safety, especially in this patient:\n\n1. **Clinical vs instrument discrepancy:** Screening tools like C-SSRS capture what the patient is willing to report. Patients with borderline personality traits may:\n- Dissociate from their emotional state and genuinely not recognize ideation\n- Suppress disclosure to avoid perceived consequences (1:1 monitoring, transfer to psychiatric unit)\n- Experience rapidly fluctuating suicidal ideation that is absent at the moment of screening but present hours later\n\n2. **"Going through the motions" is concerning:** Emotional disconnection, flat affect, and passive compliance can represent:\n- **Dissociation:** A coping mechanism but also a risk state — dissociated patients can act impulsively without apparent forewarning\n- **Resigned hopelessness:** More dangerous than active distress — patients who have "given up" may not endorse ideation because they do not see a future worth saving\n- **Therapeutic suppression:** She may be complying with rehab to avoid consequences rather than because she sees value in recovery\n\n3. **Recommended response:**\n- Do NOT rely solely on C-SSRS scores for safety decisions\n- Increase frequency of informal therapeutic contact — daily psychology check-ins, not just structured assessments\n- Observe for behavioral warning signs: giving away possessions, sudden calmness after period of distress, withdrawal from relationships, searching for methods online\n- Maintain current safety precautions regardless of screening tool results\n- Staff who interact with her daily (nurses, aides, therapists) are often the first to notice changes — create a formal communication pathway for them to report concerns\n- Reassess medications — if her affect is this flat on fluoxetine + quetiapine, consider whether the medications are contributing to emotional blunting vs treating depression',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ Challenge question',
      content:
          '**Q: The patient asks her physical therapist, "What\'s the highest floor in this hospital?" The therapist is unsure if this is a safety concern or a casual question. How should this be handled?**\n\n**A:** This statement must be treated as a potential safety concern until proven otherwise, given the patient\'s history of jumping from a height:\n\n1. **Immediate therapist response:** The therapist should respond calmly and directly without showing alarm: "I noticed you asked about the highest floor. Can you help me understand what made you think of that?" This opens the conversation without accusation.\n\n2. **Regardless of her answer,** the therapist must:\n- Document the statement verbatim in the medical record\n- Report immediately to the treating physician and nursing\n- Report to the psychology/psychiatry team\n\n3. **Clinical assessment within the hour:** A member of the psychiatric care team (psychologist or psychiatrist) should conduct a focused risk assessment. The question has specific relevance because her method of attempt was jumping from a height — asking about high floors could represent:\n- **Method fixation:** Planning or fantasizing about repeating her attempt\n- **Trauma processing:** Intrusive thoughts about the attempt without active intent\n- **Casual inquiry:** Less likely given her specific history but possible\n\n4. **Safety response:** Until the assessment is completed, implement enhanced safety measures: 1:1 observation, restrict access to stairwells and upper floors, ensure windows are secured.\n\n5. **Staff education point:** In post-suicide attempt rehabilitation, ALL seemingly innocent statements about heights, falls, access to means, or "getting out" should be reported and assessed. It is far better to over-report and find no concern than to dismiss a genuine warning sign. Staff should never feel they are "overreacting" for reporting.\n\n6. **Create a culture of reporting:** Frame reporting as "caring about the patient," not "getting the patient in trouble." The patient herself may be ambivalently reaching out for help through this statement — missing it would be a failure of the system.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT',
      content:
          '**SCI Rehab with Concurrent Psychiatric Care:**\n- **Dual-track rehabilitation model:** Standard T12 paraplegia rehabilitation program (wheelchair mobility, transfers, ADLs, bowel/bladder management) running concurrently with intensive psychiatric/psychological treatment\n- **Therapy schedule adaptation:** Schedule rehab therapy sessions when the patient is most engaged (may not be early morning if sedated from quetiapine). Allow flexibility for therapy cancellations on high-distress days without punitive documentation.\n- **Trauma-informed approach:** All staff interactions should be trauma-informed. Avoid power struggles, respect her sense of control, explain all interventions before performing them, obtain verbal consent for hands-on therapy.\n\n**1:1 Monitoring Protocol:**\n- Continuous 1:1 observation during acute risk period (first 1-2 weeks minimum)\n- Trained sitter (not just nursing aide) who understands suicide precaution behaviors\n- Sitter must maintain line-of-sight at ALL times including bathroom (with privacy curtain but line of sight to feet)\n- De-escalation protocol: if the patient becomes agitated, sitter calls for nursing/behavioral health support rather than physical restraint (restraint is retraumatizing)\n- Gradual step-down: transition from 1:1 to every-15-minute checks only after formal reassessment by psychiatry\n\n**Sharps Restrictions and Environmental Safety:**\n- Room safety sweep: remove sharps containers from room, secure IV poles, remove plastic bags, ensure no ligature points (shower heads, curtain rods), window locks confirmed\n- No sharps at bedside: razors, scissors, nail clippers, glass cups\n- Medications dispensed one dose at a time by nursing — no bedside medication storage\n- Personal items searched (with patient\'s knowledge and consent when possible) for sharps, hoarded medications, or other potential means\n- Monitor for improvised self-harm: pen caps, broken plastic, abrasive surfaces — patients with self-injury history are resourceful\n\n**Therapy Engagement Strategies:**\n- **Do not force participation** — forced compliance increases resistance and damages the therapeutic alliance\n- **Motivational approach:** Find one thing she cares about and build around it. If she values independence: "Learning to transfer means you can leave a room whenever you want." If she values academics: "We can set up your laptop so you can complete your last 6 credits from here."\n- **Small goals:** Frame daily rehab goals as choices, not mandates. "Today you can work on wheelchair skills or transfer training — which would you prefer?"\n- **Peer support:** If available, connect with a young adult SCI peer mentor (NOT another post-suicide attempt patient — shared normalization of suicidality is dangerous)\n- **Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) integration:** Given her BPD traits and self-injury history, DBT skills (distress tolerance, emotional regulation, mindfulness) should be incorporated daily by psychology',
