    Section(
      title: '+ Management question',
      content:
          '**Q: During her third week, nursing discovers superficial cuts on her left forearm made with a broken plastic cup. She is calm and says, "It\'s not a suicide thing. It helps me feel something. I\'ve done this since I was 14." How do you respond clinically?**\n\n**A:** Non-suicidal self-injury (NSSI) in this context requires a calibrated response that takes the behavior seriously without overreacting:\n\n1. **Immediate medical care:** Assess the wounds — clean, dress, photograph for documentation. Determine if any wounds require sutures. Check tetanus status.\n\n2. **Remove the means:** Environmental sweep of her room for broken or sharp objects. Review all items provided to the patient.\n\n3. **Distinguish NSSI from suicidal behavior (but do not dismiss):**\n- Her statement is likely accurate — NSSI and suicidal behavior are distinct phenomena. NSSI is used for emotional regulation (feeling something when dissociated, releasing emotional pain, regaining control).\n- HOWEVER, NSSI is a significant risk factor for future suicide attempts, and in a patient with a recent high-lethality attempt, it signals that her emotional regulation is decompensating.\n- This is NOT "just cutting" — it is a red flag that her current psychiatric treatment is insufficient.\n\n4. **Clinical response:**\n- **Increase psychiatric involvement:** Urgent psychiatry reassessment. Consider medication adjustment (is fluoxetine effective? Is the dose adequate? Consider adding or switching medications).\n- **Intensify DBT skills training:** Daily distress tolerance skills practice. Teach replacement behaviors: ice cubes on skin, red marker on arms, intense physical sensation (holding ice, cold shower) as alternatives to cutting.\n- **Create a safety plan collaboratively WITH her:** Use Stanley and Brown\'s Safety Planning Intervention. Include her warning signs, coping strategies, reasons for living, and contacts for help.\n- **Do NOT punish or shame:** Confiscating privileges, expressing disappointment, or implementing restrictive measures as consequences will worsen the behavior. NSSI is a coping mechanism — removing it without replacing it leaves her with no emotional outlet.\n\n5. **Communication with her:** "I believe you that this helps you manage overwhelming feelings. I am not angry and you are not in trouble. But I am concerned because it tells me you are hurting more than the current plan is addressing. I want to work with you and the psychiatrist to find safer ways to manage those feelings. Can we do that together?"',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE',
      content:
          '**Inpatient Psychiatry Coordination:**\n- **Consultation-liaison model:** Psychiatry provides regular (at minimum twice-weekly) follow-up during rehab admission. This should be a scheduled, predictable service — not just crisis-driven.\n- **Psychology:** Daily sessions during initial high-risk period, transitioning to 3-4x/week as stability improves. DBT-informed approach is ideal. Trauma processing (the attempt itself, the resulting SCI) should begin only when the patient is stabilized — premature trauma processing can destabilize.\n- **Medication management by psychiatry,** not physiatry. Coordinate medication changes to avoid impacts on rehabilitation participation.\n- **Communication structure:** Daily huddle between rehab team and psych team. Use a shared safety communication tool (e.g., structured handoff at shift change including current risk level).\n\n**Lethal Means Restriction:**\n- **Environmental controls:** Already described (room safety sweep, sharps restriction, medication control)\n- **Discharge planning for means restriction:**\n  - Home assessment before discharge: remove or secure firearms (if any), medications (all medications in a locked box managed by a designated person, not the patient), sharp objects, access to heights\n  - Lethal means counseling with the patient AND her mother: educate on the importance of means restriction as a life-saving intervention\n  - She should NOT live alone after discharge — arrange for mother or roommate cohabitation during the highest-risk period (3-6 months post-attempt)\n\n**Outpatient Safety Planning:**\n- **Crisis contacts:** 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline, Crisis Text Line (text HOME to 741741), local mobile crisis team number, her psychiatrist\'s after-hours number\n- **Safety plan:** Completed before discharge using the Stanley-Brown Safety Planning Intervention. Physical copies to patient, mother, and outpatient providers.\n- **Outpatient psychiatric follow-up within 72 hours of discharge** — the post-discharge period is the highest risk for completed suicide. Arrange this before discharge, not as a recommendation.\n- **Outpatient therapist:** Resume care with her pre-existing DBT therapist if possible (therapeutic relationship continuity is valuable). If not, arrange a warm handoff to a new therapist.\n- **SCI follow-up:** Physiatry follow-up in 2 weeks. SCI-specific support group. Ensure continuity of bowel/bladder supplies and equipment.\n- **Academic re-engagement:** If she wants to complete her degree, occupational therapy can facilitate adaptive technology setup and communication with the university disability services office.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ Systems question',
      content:
          '**Q: Your rehab unit does not have a locked unit, 1:1 sitters are expensive and in short supply, and the nursing staff is uncomfortable caring for a suicidal patient. How do you address these systemic barriers?**\n\n**A:** These are real, common barriers that must be addressed proactively:\n\n1. **1:1 sitter resources:** If institutional sitters are unavailable, explore alternatives: family member (mother) as supervised companion during daytime hours (with training on what to observe and report), nursing student clinical placement focused on psychiatric nursing, telehealth-based virtual sitter technology (camera monitoring with centralized monitoring staff). Insurance authorization for 1:1 should be pursued — document medical necessity based on recent high-lethality attempt and ongoing passive suicidal ideation.\n\n2. **Unlocked unit safety:** Implement environmental modifications: assign a room closest to the nursing station, ensure visual line of sight from the hallway, use bed alarm for nocturnal monitoring, restrict access to exits (programming her wheelchair to require staff assistance for door access if possible), and implement structured check-in protocols (every 15 minutes at minimum).\n\n3. **Staff discomfort:** This is the most important barrier to address because it directly affects patient care:\n- **Education session:** Provide focused training for the rehab team on caring for post-suicide-attempt patients. Cover: risk assessment basics, warning signs, therapeutic communication, what to do when a patient makes concerning statements, documentation requirements.\n- **Reduce fear through structure:** Staff are uncomfortable because they feel untrained and responsible for preventing suicide. Provide clear protocols: "If the patient says X, do Y. If you observe Z, call the physician." Structure reduces anxiety.\n- **Normalize the clinical scenario:** Post-suicide-attempt rehabilitation is an expected part of SCI care — up to 10% of traumatic SCI results from self-harm. This is not an unusual case; it is a predictable one that the unit should be prepared for.\n- **Debriefing and support:** Schedule weekly team debriefs for staff caring for this patient. Acknowledge the emotional toll. Provide access to employee assistance program (EAP) for staff who are struggling.\n\n4. **If the patient decompensates to active suicidal crisis:** Have a clear transfer protocol to an inpatient psychiatric unit. Ensure the receiving unit can manage her SCI needs (wheelchair accessibility, bowel/bladder program). This may require a medical-psychiatric dual unit or creative co-management arrangement.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          '**Scenario:** During a physical therapy session on day 12, the patient stops mid-transfer and says quietly to the therapist: "What\'s the point of any of this? I wish I had died. At least then I wouldn\'t be stuck in a wheelchair forever."\n\n**Immediate Response Protocol While Building Therapeutic Rapport:**\n\n**What the therapist should do immediately:**\n\n1. **Stop the therapy activity.** This statement takes priority over the session. Put down any equipment. Give the patient your full attention.\n\n2. **Respond with calm, non-judgmental presence:**\n"I heard what you said, and I am not going to pretend I didn\'t. What you just shared matters to me. Can you tell me more about what you are feeling right now?"\n\n3. **Do NOT:**\n- Panic or show alarm (this makes the patient feel her feelings are dangerous and she should hide them)\n- Say "Don\'t say that" or "You don\'t mean that" (invalidating)\n- Launch into a motivational speech about recovery potential (premature and dismissive)\n- Immediately leave to call someone (this feels like abandonment and punishment)\n- Share personal stories of adversity or compare her suffering to others\n\n4. **Listen actively.** Let her talk. She may have more to say. Silence is acceptable. Do not rush to fill it.\n\n5. **Validate without agreeing:**\n"It makes sense that you feel this way. You are dealing with something incredibly painful — both the injury and everything that led to it. Feeling like this is overwhelming and wanting an escape from it is understandable."\n\n6. **Assess for immediacy:**\n"When you say you wish you had died — are you telling me that you are thinking about hurting yourself right now, or are you telling me about how much pain you are in?"\n\n*If she endorses active ideation with plan or intent: activate emergency protocol — stay with her, call for help, do not leave her alone.*\n\n*If she describes pain/hopelessness without active intent: continue the conversation.*\n\n7. **Build the bridge to support:**\n"Thank you for telling me. I know it is not easy to say something like that out loud. I am going to stay with you right now, and I would also like to have [psychologist/psychiatrist name] come talk with you today — not because you are in trouble, but because what you are feeling deserves more support than I can offer in a PT session. Is that okay?"\n\n8. **After the immediate interaction:**\n- Document the statement verbatim in the medical record\n- Notify the attending physiatrist, psychology, and nursing immediately\n- Update the C-SSRS assessment\n- Review and update the safety plan\n- Continue 1:1 monitoring per protocol\n\n**For the attending physiatrist (you):**\n- When you see the patient later that day, do NOT pretend you do not know what she said\n- "I heard you had a hard moment during PT today. I wanted to check in with you about it."\n- This communicates that the team is connected and she is not being surveilled — she is being cared for\n- Reassess medication regimen with psychiatry\n- Consider whether rehab intensity needs temporary reduction to match her psychological capacity',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ Communication question',
      content:
          '**Q: Her mother calls you in tears: "I blame myself for this. If I had been a better mother, she wouldn\'t have jumped. Now she\'s paralyzed because of me. What am I supposed to do?" How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** Caregiver guilt after a loved one\'s suicide attempt is nearly universal and requires its own therapeutic response:\n\n"I can hear how much pain you are in, and I want to take a moment to address what you just said, because it is important.\n\nYou are not the reason your daughter jumped. Suicide attempts are the result of a complex interaction of mental illness, emotional crisis, and overwhelming pain — they are not caused by parenting. Your daughter has a serious psychiatric illness that she has been struggling with since adolescence. Depression and borderline personality disorder are brain-based conditions, not reflections of how she was raised.\n\nI also want to point out something that tells me a great deal about your relationship: she called 911 herself within minutes of jumping. That act — reaching out for help in the worst moment of her life — tells me she has a connection to life and to the people who love her. That connection does not develop without a parent who cares.\n\nNow, let me address your question — what are you supposed to do:\n\n1. **First, take care of yourself.** You cannot support your daughter through this recovery if you are drowning in guilt and grief. I am going to ask our social worker to connect you with a support group for family members of suicide attempt survivors. I also strongly recommend individual counseling for you — not because something is wrong with you, but because what you are going through is traumatic.\n\n2. **Be present without being perfect.** Your daughter does not need you to fix this. She needs you to show up consistently, to sit with her even when she does not want to talk, and to let her know you are not going anywhere. You do not have to say the right thing — just being there says enough.\n\n3. **Learn about her conditions.** Understanding depression and borderline personality disorder will help you make sense of her behaviors without personalizing them. The National Alliance on Mental Illness (NAMI) has a Family-to-Family program that is excellent.\n\n4. **Help with the practical things.** Right now, the most tangible ways you can help are: attending family therapy sessions when invited, learning her bowel and bladder program so you can assist at home, helping prepare her apartment for wheelchair accessibility, and being her advocate with insurance and disability paperwork.\n\n5. **Set boundaries when needed.** Supporting your daughter through recovery does not mean sacrificing your own well-being. It is okay to ask for breaks, to say you are overwhelmed, and to set limits on what you can provide.\n\nYou are already doing something right — you are here, you are asking how to help, and you clearly love your daughter deeply. That matters more than you know."',
    ),
